1 RUN
    The RUN command loads a VAX/VMS executable image into memory, activates
    the shareable images it was linked against, and runs it.  This command
    assumes that a VMINIT has been run, and the standard micro-kernel
    (kernel.asm) has been loaded and initialized.  The shareable images are
    activated as VMS's image activator activates images that aren't
    installed (see "Shareable images" below).

    The image is started at its transfer address.  A shareable image (one
    LINK/SHAREABLE made) has none, and running one is %DCL-E-NOTFR, "no
    transfer address", as on VMS.

    If the optional /DEBUG qualifier is used, the image is loaded and then run
    under the debugger, which stops it at the first instruction of its main
    routine (after the entry mask) and prompts with DBG>.  GO continues it;
    STEP, SET BREAK, EXAMINE, and the rest are the debugger's commands.  An
    image linked with /DEBUG (LINK/DEBUG) starts the debugger the same way,
    unless RUN/NODEBUG says not to.  An image linked /NOTRACEBACK has no symbols for
    the debugger, and runs without it.  /STEP and /BREAK are
    synonyms of /DEBUG, and /NOEXECUTE loads the image without running it.

    Syntax:

        RUN [/DEBUG|/NODEBUG] [/NOEXECUTE] <image-name>[/HOST]

    Parameter:

        <image-name>    An image file, on the native system or on a mounted
                        ODS-2 volume, by the same rules as MACRO's and LINK's
                        files (see HELP MACRO): a VMS file specification
                        (DUA0:[PROGS]HELLO.EXE), or a bare name while SET
                        DEFAULT is on a volume, is a file on the volume, with
                        the type EXE if it has none.  Anything else, or any
                        name followed by /HOST, is a native file.  An image
                        made on a VAX can be copied to the native system in
                        binary mode, or read in place from its disk.

                        If the native file system is case sensitive, the name may
                        need to be enclosed in quotes to preserve case.

    Shareable images:

    Each shareable image the program names (in its shareable image list,
    and those images' own lists in turn) is found, checked, and placed in
    the process's P0 space before the program runs.  An image is found
    by these rules, in order:

        1. An image govax stands for (LIBRTL, LIBRTL2, MTHRTL, the system's
           run-time libraries, ...) is govax's own routines, unless a
           logical name of the image's own name is defined: DEFINE
           LIBRTL DUA1:[X]LIBRTL.EXE asks for that file instead.
        2. The image's name parsed with the default SYS$SHARE:.EXE, as
           VMS's activator does: a logical name of the image's name
           (DEFINE MYSHR DUA0:[LIB]MYSHR.EXE) leads to its file, with any
           fields it lacks taken from SYS$SHARE; otherwise SYS$SHARE does,
           each place of a search list in turn.  With a system volume,
           its STARTUP.COM defines SYS$SHARE as its [SYSTEM.SYSLIB]
           directory, where FTNRTL.EXE is; define it as a search list, in
           SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM say, to keep shareable images of
           your own on another volume too.
        3. The host: the host library directory (the vax.library
           setting), then the name as given, each as written and then in
           lower case.

    An image no rule finds stops the activation, as on VMS:

        %DCL-W-ACTIMAGE, error activating image MYSHR
        -CLI-E-IMAGEFNF, image file not found DUA0:[SYSLIB]MYSHR.EXE;

    (here with SYS$SHARE defined as DUA0:[SYSLIB]), and $STATUS %X100388B2.

    The image's ident and match control (LINK's GSMATCH= option) are
    checked against those the program was linked with.  The two match
    controls must agree, and then the program's decides: ALWAYS matches
    any ident, EQUAL needs the same major and minor ids, and LEQUAL the
    same major id and a minor id no lower than the program's.  A mismatch
    is %SYSTEM-F-SHRIDMISMAT, with the image's file named.  govax's own
    routines always match.

    A shareable image linked at a base address (BASE=) goes there, as does
    one the program's link placed (an executable linked with BASE=); any
    other goes at the end of P0's pages, the shareable image list taken
    from its end, so the program's last image is placed first.  Then each
    image's fixups are done (its .ADDRESS list and its G^ cells, and those
    of the program), and its pages take their protections.  Shareable
    images are private to the process: copied into memory, or mapped
    from their files with paging on (VMINIT/PAGING; see HELP VMINIT), never
    shared with another process.

    The debugger's SHOW IMAGE lists the images and the files they came
    from, and its HELP SHOW SHIM and HELP ASM PSEUDO SHIM describe how
    govax's own routines are reached.

    RUN gives the image no command text: LIB$GET_FOREIGN returns an empty
    string.  To pass an image text, run it as a foreign command (see HELP
    FOREIGN), or give the text after the file name on govax's own command
    line ("govax run forth.exe 2 3 + .").
